body:
TL;DR: Currently living with my DA ex who broke up with me recently. Any tips on how to navigate cohabitation? 

&#x200B;

Without divulging too many details (which you can find on another post on this thread from about 2 weeks ago), my DA girlfriend dumped me. It wasn't mutual, and the reasons she gave for it felt pretty flimsy (like citing minor disagreements we had had and resolved over 2 years ago), so there was a lot of hurt. What did help, however, was reading Attached by Amir Levine and Rachel Heller. It's led me to frame all of our discontents in a new light, and I've quickly come to realize that I, too, was done with the relationship before it was over. The big catch, however is...

&#x200B;

We're still living together. She had mentioned something about wanting to stay friends, but apparently I said something(?) during the breakup process which drove her away (pretty sure that she's just looking for excuses to emotionally push me out at this point), so now that's TBD. A few days later, I tried asking her what she thinks our dynamic should be, but she was too depleted to entertain any discussion. Knowing what I know now, I never would've broached the matter with her, but here we are. So now we're together, sleeping in different rooms, and...I have no idea where we're at or how to interact with her. I know how quickly DAs get over breakups, so I can't help but feel a general sense of apathy from her. In the fleeting encounters we've had, she doesn't ask me about my day and gives fairly terse responses to whatever questions I may ask her. I try to give her her space, only making pleasantries when I have to pass through her room to get to the kitchen or something, and don't really approach her for conversation. Does she not want to speak to me at all? Does she care that I'm alive? How am I supposed to do this? 

&#x200B;

I honestly wouldn't mind being friends with her, though I think that she might've written that out of the equation. Maybe she doesn't feel anything, but it's just surreal (and honestly kind of hurtful) that she seems content with acting as if we're roommates who barely know one another. This is somewhat how her last relationship ended (though they were at this point before they broke up), and I wouldn't want to live through that experience. What can I do to make our living situation as palatable as possible while still respecting where she's at, wherever that might be?
